# tools/e/encoding_base64_witness.rish -- Base64 (RFC 4648) authored in pure Rye: the binary-to-text armor a key, a signature, or a sealed
# frame travels in, proven against the RFC 4648 §10 known-answers and byte-for-byte against an independent implementation (encoding - the
# serialization sibling of Season G - Cryptography).
#
# Season: encoding -- the text-form sibling of the crypto library. Where crypto/ authors the mathematics that produces bytes, encoding/
# authors the text those bytes travel in, keeping the same parity discipline: authored from the standard, calling no std.base64 in the
# primitive, proven byte-for-byte against a reference runnable now.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the wave that seats the Rye-first spine).
# Clean-room: the alphabet and padding law are RFC 4648's own; the arithmetic is ours (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# encoding/base64.rye authors Base64 from RFC 4648 -- the standard and URL-safe alphabets, the '=' padding law, the three-byte/four-symbol
# packing and its inverse. The primitive calls no std.base64. Parity is proven at selftest against two references runnable now: the RFC
# 4648 §10 known-answers ("" - "f" - "fo" - "foo" - "foob" - "fooba" - "foobar") match text for text and round-trip back, and Zig's own
# std.base64 as an independent implementation, byte-for-byte across every remainder class for payloads 0..64 bytes, both alphabets. A bad
# character, a bad length, and a too-small destination each refuse their named error before a partial result escapes. Purely local
# arithmetic -- no key, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/e/encoding_base64_witness.rish

say "encoding-base64: Language EN — Base64 (RFC 4648) is the binary-to-text armor every wire format and text-safe artifact leans on: a signed-carry frame in a Bron field, a vault-sealed blob in a note, a public key or a content address rendered for a human. The crypto library proves the bytes; this module renders them to text and back, byte-for-byte, so a hand can carry a key or a seal through any printable-only channel and recover it exactly. Authoring our own, parity-checked, calling no std.base64 is the same discipline Season G keeps for the mathematics beneath."
say "encoding-base64: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: three eight-bit bytes pack into four six-bit symbols indexed from a 64-character alphabet; the standard form (A–Z a–z 0–9 + /) pads a short final group to a multiple of four with '=', the URL-safe form (A–Z a–z 0–9 - _) is written unpadded; decoding inverts the map so the same bytes always render to the same text and the same text always recovers the same bytes."
say "encoding-base64: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.base64; it is authored from the standard, bounded input (max_decoded_bytes, max_encoded_bytes), contract asserts on every function, buffer-length checked before the first write. Parity holds against the RFC 4648 §10 known-answers AND Zig's independent std.base64 byte-for-byte across every remainder class, both alphabets; a bad character refuses BadChar, a bad length refuses BadLength, a too-small destination refuses DestTooSmall — each before a partial result escapes. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "encoding/bin/base64"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p encoding/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build encoding/base64.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "encoding-base64: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "encoding/bin/base64.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "encoding-base64: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "encoding-base64: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN encoding-base64' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "encoding-base64: selftest did not print the GREEN encoding-base64 line"
